 {
  "source": "Barclays",
  "title": "The new geo-economics of energy",
  "type": "macro",
  "priority": "medium",
  "published": "2026-09-11",
  "insights": [
   "Energy has become a central channel for modern geo-economic power plays, shifting from efficiency-driven trade to security-focused, fragmented supply chains.",
   "The US has transformed from a net oil importer to a net exporter, while Europe faces increased reliance on imports following the exit from nuclear and loss of Russian pipeline gas.",
   "China maintains a quasi-monopolistic position in critical mineral refining (e.g., rare earths, graphite), providing significant geopolitical leverage that Western nations are struggling to decouple from.",
   "Electricity demand is surging, driven by AI data center needs, necessitating a 'grid capex supercycle' with a potential $600bn investment opportunity.",
